['```
void copy_bytes(void* dest, const void* src, size_t num_bytes) {
  char* dst = (char*)dest;
  const char* src_ptr = (const char*)src;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < num_bytes; ++i) {
    *dst++ = *src_ptr++;
  }
}
```
This code implements a function called `copy_bytes` that takes three arguments:

* `dest`: The destination memory location where the bytes will be copied. This argument must be a pointer to a block of memory that is large enough to hold the number of bytes specified in the third argument.
* `src`: The source memory location from which the bytes will be copied. This argument must be a pointer to a block of memory that contains the number of bytes specified in the third argument.
* `num_bytes`: The number of bytes to copy from the source to the destination.

The function first casts both arguments to `char*` pointers, which allows us to treat them as if they were single bytes. It then uses a for loop to iterate over each byte and copies it from the source to the destination using the pointer arithmetic operators `*dst++` and `*src_ptr++`. The `++i` operator increments the counter variable `i` at the end of each iteration, which is necessary because we want to keep track of how many bytes have been copied.

Note that this code assumes that the source and destination memory locations are not overlapping, meaning that copying data from one location to another will not overwrite any part of the original data before it has been fully copied. If you need to handle overlapping copies, you may need to use a different approach.']

