['
Here's a possible implementation of `atoi()` in C:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int atoi(const char* str) {
    int result = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < strlen(str); i++) {
        if (!isdigit(str[i])) {
            return -1;
        }
        result = result * 10 + (str[i] - '0');
    }
    return result;
}
```
This implementation uses a loop to iterate through each character in the input string, checking if it is a digit and then multiplying the current result by 10 and adding the value of the digit. If any non-digit characters are encountered, the function returns -1.

To use this function, you would call it with the command line arguments as follows:
```
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    for (int i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        int num = atoi(argv[i]);
        printf("Argument %d is %d\n", i, num);
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code iterates through each argument passed to the program and calls `atoi()` on each one. If the conversion is successful, it prints out the converted integer value along with the argument number. If any of the conversions fail, it returns -1.']

