#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

void execute_command(const char* command) {
    // Create a buffer to hold the output of the command
    char *output = malloc(1024);
    if (output == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to allocate memory for command output\n");
        return;
    }

    // Execute the command and store the output in the buffer
    FILE *pipe = popen(command, "r");
    if (pipe == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to execute command: %s\n", command);
        free(output);
        return;
    }

    // Read the output of the command from the pipe
    while (!feof(pipe)) {
        if (fgets(output, 1024, pipe) == NULL) {
            break;
        }
    }

    // Close the pipe and free the memory allocated for the output buffer
    pclose(pipe);
    free(output);
}

int main() {
    char *command = "ls -l";
    execute_command(command);
    return 0;
}
```
This code uses the `popen` function to execute the command and read its output in a loop. The `feof` function is used to check if the end of the file has been reached, and the `fgets` function is used to read lines from the pipe into a buffer. Finally, the `pclose` function is used to close the pipe and free any memory allocated for the output buffer.

You can also use `system` function instead of `popen`, but it has some limitations. `system` function executes the command in a separate process and returns its exit status, so you won't be able to read its output directly. Here is an example of how you might use `system`:
```
